// Authored content for 3.1.10.1 The concept of a species (OxfordAQA 9610 v5.1, printed page 19; statements
// 3.1.10.1.s1–s4 in data/spec.js). Batch 9, owned by the 3.1.10 author.
// Scope: species as the basic unit of classification; the biological species definition (largest group of organisms
// capable of interbreeding and producing fertile offspring); binomial names; and, as a skill, recognising the difficulty
// of defining species and examining the definition critically when given data about specific examples.
// Other species concepts, ring species and the three domain names are NOT required: they appear only as labelled
// supporting or extension material and no required mark depends on them. Data marked "illustrative" are invented for
// practice and say so on the page; facts about named real organisms are given with their sources in the author report.
window.BIO = window.BIO || {}; window.BIO.content = window.BIO.content || {};
(function () {
  const A = window.BIO.speciesArt;
  const R = "3.1.10.1";
  const S = (...n) => n.map((k) => `${R}.s${k}`);
  const P = (slot) => window.BIO.views.photo(slot);
  const ILL = '<span class="badge rose">illustrative data</span>';

  // ---- Species Concept Challenge: unfamiliar scenarios with data; one marked question each ----
  const scen = (k, label, data, stem, options, answer, explanation, st = S(4, 2)) => ({ label, questions: [{ id: `q-${R}-cc${k}`, ref: R, stmts: st, type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: `<div class="notice small">${data}</div>${stem}`, options, answer, explanation }] });
  const challenge = [
    scen(1, "Horse and donkey", "<strong>Data.</strong> Horses (<em>Equus caballus</em>, 2n = 64) and donkeys (<em>Equus asinus</em>, 2n = 62) are mated on farms. Their offspring, mules and hinnies, have 2n = 63. Almost all are sterile; only a little over 60 cases of a female mule producing a foal have been documented since 1527.",
      "Can the biological species definition be applied, and what does it show?",
      ["Yes: they interbreed, but the offspring are almost always infertile, so horses and donkeys are separate species", "Yes: they can interbreed, so horses and donkeys are one species", "No: the definition cannot be used because the two animals look different", "Yes: because a few female mules are fertile, horses and donkeys are one species"], 0,
      "The definition needs <em>fertile</em> offspring. Mules and hinnies are (almost always) sterile because horse and donkey chromosomes cannot all pair in meiosis, so no lasting flow of genes between horses and donkeys is possible. A very rare fertile female mule does not change this conclusion.", S(2, 4)),
    scen(2, "All-female lizard", "<strong>Data.</strong> Every New Mexico whiptail lizard (<em>Aspidoscelis neomexicana</em>) ever examined is female. Females lay eggs that develop without fertilisation, and the offspring are genetically almost identical to their mother.",
      "Can the biological species definition be applied to this lizard?",
      ["Yes: the lizards produce fertile offspring, so they meet the definition", "No: they reproduce only asexually, so there is no interbreeding to test; they must be classified using other features such as body structure and DNA", "Yes: all the lizards are female, so they cannot be a species", "No: lizards cannot be classified"], 1,
      "The definition is about interbreeding. An organism that reproduces only asexually does not mate with other members of its species, so the test cannot be applied at all. The same problem arises for bacteria and for many plants that set seed without fertilisation."),
    scen(3, "Fossil trilobites", `${ILL} <strong>Data.</strong> Fossil trilobites from two rock layers about two million years apart differ in eye shape and in the number of body segments. Intermediate forms are found in the layers between.`,
      "What is the main difficulty in deciding whether the two forms belong to one species?",
      ["They are too old to have had genes", "They are extinct, so whether they could interbreed and produce fertile offspring can never be tested; the decision must rest on structure", "Trilobites reproduced asexually", "The two forms are definitely one species because intermediates exist"], 1,
      "Extinct organisms cannot be bred. Palaeontologists group fossils by their structure, and where intermediates exist the point at which one species is said to become another is a matter of judgement."),
    scen(4, "Chihuahua and Great Dane", "<strong>Data.</strong> A Chihuahua (about 2 kg) and a Great Dane (about 60 kg) cannot mate naturally. Each breed, however, produces fertile offspring with dogs of intermediate size, and all domestic dogs are classified as one species.",
      "Which is the best evaluation of the species definition in this case?",
      ["Physical incompatibility prevents these two breeds mating, so a literal reading of the definition fails; they are kept in one species because genes can pass between them through dogs of intermediate size", "The two breeds must be separate species because they cannot interbreed", "The definition works perfectly: any two dogs can mate", "Dogs reproduce asexually, so the definition does not apply"], 0,
      "Body size is a physical barrier to mating between these two breeds, yet they belong to one interbreeding population: genes flow between them through intermediate dogs. The definition has to be read as 'capable of interbreeding' across the whole group, not between every pair of individuals."),
    scen(5, "Two look-alike warblers", "<strong>Data.</strong> The chiffchaff (<em>Phylloscopus collybita</em>) and the willow warbler (<em>Phylloscopus trochilus</em>) are small greenish birds so alike that birdwatchers usually identify them by their very different songs. Both breed in the same European woodlands, but hybrids are rare.",
      "What does this example show?",
      ["They must be one species because they look the same", "The definition works here and shows two species: in nature they rarely interbreed, partly because each responds to its own song; appearance alone would have misled", "The definition cannot be applied because they are birds", "They are one species because they share a genus"], 1,
      "Species are not defined by appearance. These birds share a habitat but do not normally interbreed: behaviour (song) helps keep them apart. Sharing the genus <em>Phylloscopus</em> shows they are closely related, not that they are one species."),
    scen(6, "Polar bear and brown bear", "<strong>Data.</strong> Polar bears (<em>Ursus maritimus</em>) and brown (grizzly) bears (<em>Ursus arctos</em>) both have 2n = 74. Since 2006, DNA testing of wild bears in the Canadian Arctic has identified several hybrids, including bears whose mother was herself a hybrid.",
      "What do these data show about the species definition?",
      ["Nothing: the hybrids must be sterile", "Some recognised species produce fertile hybrids, so the definition does not always draw a sharp line; the two are still treated as separate species because they differ greatly in body, ecology and DNA and usually breed separately", "Polar bears and brown bears are now officially one species", "The definition cannot be applied to bears because they live in the Arctic"], 1,
      "A hybrid that has offspring of its own is fertile, so by the definition alone these bears could be one species. Biologists keep them separate because they normally live and breed apart and differ in many features and in their DNA. Fertile hybrids between recognised species are a real limitation of the definition."),
    scen(7, "Island frogs", `${ILL} <strong>Data.</strong> Two populations of a frog live on islands 300 km apart. In a laboratory, frogs from the two islands mate and produce fertile offspring. In the wild, frogs from the two islands never meet.`,
      "Which is the best evaluation?",
      ["They are definitely one species", "They are definitely two species", "The definition cannot be tested in natural conditions because the populations are geographically separated; a laboratory result may not show whether they would interbreed in the wild", "The definition cannot be applied because frogs reproduce asexually"], 2,
      "Geographically separated populations never have the chance to interbreed, so the test cannot be carried out in nature. In captivity, animals sometimes mate with partners they would ignore in the wild, so laboratory results must be interpreted with care."),
    scen(8, "Neanderthals", "<strong>Data.</strong> Neanderthals are known from fossils and from DNA extracted from their bones. They became extinct about 40 000 years ago. Present-day people whose ancestors lived outside Africa carry about 2 % of DNA inherited from Neanderthals.",
      "What do these data suggest?",
      ["Neanderthals and modern humans never met", "Neanderthals and modern humans interbred and at least some offspring were fertile; because Neanderthals are extinct the definition can only be applied indirectly, and scientists disagree about whether they were a separate species", "Neanderthals were certainly a separate species because they are extinct", "Neanderthal DNA came from asexual reproduction"], 1,
      "DNA passed down to present-day people shows that some hybrid offspring were fertile. Many scientists still use the name <em>Homo neanderthalensis</em>; others treat Neanderthals as a subspecies of <em>Homo sapiens</em>. This is a genuine scientific disagreement, which is exactly the difficulty the specification asks you to recognise."),
    scen(9, "Lions and tigers", "<strong>Data.</strong> Lions (<em>Panthera leo</em>) and tigers (<em>Panthera tigris</em>) both have 2n = 38. Today they rarely if ever meet in the wild. In captivity they have produced hybrids (ligers and tigons). Male hybrids are sterile; some female hybrids have produced cubs.",
      "Which is the best evaluation?",
      ["Lions and tigers are one species because some hybrids are fertile", "The data give no clear answer from the definition alone (only some hybrids are fertile, and only in captivity); lions and tigers are treated as separate species because in nature they do not interbreed", "Ligers prove that the definition is always correct", "Lions and tigers cannot be classified because they live in different places"], 1,
      "Partial fertility (females fertile, males sterile) and hybrids known only from captivity are both limits of the definition. Because the two do not form a single interbreeding population in nature, they are treated as two species in one genus."),
  ];

  window.BIO.content[R] = {
    status: "draft",
    banner: "mule-hybrid", bannerText: "A mule has a horse for a mother and a donkey for a father, yet it cannot breed. Deciding where one species ends and another begins is harder than it looks.",
    keywords: ["species", "basic unit of classification", "biological species definition", "interbreed", "fertile offspring", "sterile", "hybrid", "mule", "hinny", "liger", "binomial", "binomial name", "genus", "species name", "specific epithet", "Linnaeus", "italics", "asexual reproduction", "fossil", "extinct", "geographical separation", "physical incompatibility", "behaviour", "song", "reproductive isolation", "Neanderthal", "ring species"],
    notes: () => `
<div class="notice"><strong>What the specification requires (3.1.10.1).</strong> (1) The <strong>species</strong> is the basic unit of biological classification. (2) A species may be defined as the <strong>largest group of organisms capable of interbreeding and producing fertile offspring</strong>. (3) Each species is universally identified by a <strong>binomial</strong>: the name of its genus and its species. (4) You should be able to <strong>recognise the difficulty of defining species</strong> and to <strong>examine the definition critically when you are given data</strong> about specific examples. Other ways of defining species, ring species and the names of the three domains are not required: they appear here only as labelled supporting or extension material. <span class="badge teal">required</span> <span class="badge">supporting</span> <span class="badge amber">extension</span></div>

<h2 data-stmt="${R}.s1">1. The species: the basic unit of classification <span class="badge teal">required</span></h2>
<p>Biologists sort the millions of kinds of living organisms into groups. The smallest of the main groups, and the one every organism is placed in first, is the <strong>species</strong>. Larger groups (genus, family and so on, in 3.1.10.2) are built up from species: a genus is one or more similar species, a family one or more similar genera.</p>
<ul><li>Members of one species share many features of structure, biochemistry and behaviour, because they share a common pool of genes.</li>
<li><span class="badge">supporting</span> A reason often given in textbooks is that, unlike the larger groups, a species can be tied to something that happens in nature: <strong>whether organisms breed with each other</strong>, whereas the boundaries of a genus or a family are decided by biologists. Taxonomists debate how far this makes species more "natural" than higher groups; the specification asks only that you know the species is the basic unit.</li>
<li>Counting species is how biodiversity is measured (3.1.11.2), and species are the units that conservation laws protect.</li></ul>

<h2 data-stmt="${R}.s2">2. Defining a species <span class="badge teal">required</span></h2>
<div class="notice sage"><strong>Definition.</strong> A species is the <strong>largest group of organisms capable of interbreeding and producing fertile offspring</strong>.</div>
<p>Every part of the definition matters:</p>
<table class="tbl"><thead><tr><th>Words</th><th>Why they are there</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>largest group</td><td>All the individuals that could breed together belong to one species, wherever they live. A single herd or population is only part of a species.</td></tr>
<tr><td>capable of interbreeding</td><td>Members need not actually mate; they must be <em>able</em> to. Two lions on different continents are one species.</td></tr>
<tr><td>producing <strong>fertile</strong> offspring</td><td>Offspring that are themselves able to reproduce. Horses and donkeys can interbreed, but their offspring (mules and hinnies) are almost always sterile, so horses and donkeys are <strong>separate species</strong>.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p><span class="badge">supporting</span> This is often called the <strong>biological species concept</strong>. Because members of a species interbreed, genes can spread through the whole species, which is why its members stay alike. A species is therefore <strong>reproductively isolated</strong> from other species: genes do not normally pass between them. How new species arise by reproductive isolation is A-level content (3.3.8.2).</p>
<div class="figure"><div class="fig-view">${A.muleCross()}</div></div>
<p><span class="badge">supporting</span> <strong>Why mules are sterile.</strong> A mule receives 32 chromosomes from its horse parent and 31 from its donkey parent (2n = 63). Horse and donkey chromosomes differ in number and in structure, so in the mule's meiosis (3.1.9.1) many chromosomes have no homologous partner to pair with. Meiosis breaks down and very few viable gametes form.</p>

<h2 data-stmt="${R}.s3">3. Naming species: the binomial system <span class="badge teal">required</span></h2>
<p>Each species is identified by a <strong>binomial</strong> ("two names"): the name of its <strong>genus</strong> followed by its <strong>species name</strong>. The lion is <em>Panthera leo</em>; humans are <em>Homo sapiens</em>.</p>
<div class="figure"><div class="fig-view">${A.binomial()}</div></div>
<ul><li><strong>Rules for writing a binomial.</strong> Genus name first, with a <strong>capital</strong> first letter; species name second, all in <strong>lower case</strong>. In print both words are in <em>italics</em>; when handwritten, each word is <strong>underlined</strong>. After the full name has been used once, the genus may be shortened to its initial: <em>P. leo</em>.</li>
<li>The species name on its own is not enough: <em>leo</em> alone does not identify a species, but <em>Panthera leo</em> does. <span class="badge">supporting</span> Formally, the second word is called the specific epithet; the species name is the whole binomial.</li>
<li>Species in the same genus share the first word: the tiger is <em>Panthera tigris</em>. A shared genus name shows that species are closely related.</li>
<li><strong>Why binomials, not common names?</strong> The binomial is <strong>universal</strong>: scientists everywhere, whatever their language, use the same name for the same species. Common names vary between languages and regions, and one common name can mean different species. The bird called a robin in Britain (<em>Erithacus rubecula</em>) is a different species, in a different family, from the American robin (<em>Turdus migratorius</em>).</li></ul>
<p class="small"><span class="badge">supporting</span> The system was established by the Swedish naturalist Carl Linnaeus, for plants in 1753 and for animals in 1758. Names are written in Latin or in Latinised forms of other words.</p>

<h2 data-stmt="${R}.s4 ${R}.s2">4. Why species are hard to define <span class="badge teal">required</span></h2>
<p>The definition is clear in principle but often difficult, or impossible, to apply. You should be able to recognise each of these difficulties in an unfamiliar example.</p>
<table class="tbl"><thead><tr><th>Difficulty</th><th>Example</th><th>Why the definition struggles</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Sterile hybrids</strong></td><td>Horse × donkey: mules and hinnies</td><td>The definition works (offspring not fertile, so two species), but shows that interbreeding alone is not the test: fertility must be checked.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Fertile hybrids between recognised species</strong></td><td>Polar bear × brown bear in the Canadian Arctic; grey wolf × coyote in North America; many plants</td><td>Some pairs of species that differ greatly produce fertile hybrids, so by the definition alone they could be one species.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Partly fertile hybrids</strong></td><td>Lion × tiger (ligers, tigons), known essentially from captivity: males sterile, some females fertile</td><td>Fertility is not all or nothing; the definition gives no clear answer.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Asexual reproduction</strong></td><td>Bacteria; all-female whiptail lizards; many dandelions</td><td>Members reproduce without mating with each other, so the test cannot be applied.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Extinct organisms</strong></td><td>Fossils; Neanderthals</td><td>Breeding can never be observed; decisions rest on structure (and, rarely, ancient DNA).</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Geographical separation</strong></td><td>Populations on separate islands or continents</td><td>They never have the chance to interbreed, so whether they <em>could</em> is unknown; matings in captivity may not reflect the wild.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Physical incompatibility</strong></td><td>Chihuahua and Great Dane (both <em>Canis familiaris</em>)</td><td>Size prevents mating, yet they are one species because genes flow through dogs of intermediate size.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Behaviour prevents mating</strong></td><td>Chiffchaff and willow warbler: nearly identical, different songs</td><td>Organisms that might be able to produce fertile offspring do not choose to mate (song, display, breeding at different times); deciding this needs field observation.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Variation within a species</strong></td><td>Males and females that look very different; larvae and adults</td><td>Appearance is a poor guide: look-alikes may be separate species, and very different-looking organisms may be one species.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p class="small"><span class="badge">supporting</span> On the dog's name: many references now write <em>Canis lupus familiaris</em>, treating the dog as a domesticated form of the grey wolf. Either form is acceptable here; the point is that all dogs belong to one species.</p>
<div class="notice amber"><strong>Common trap.</strong> "They can interbreed, so they are one species" is incomplete: you must also consider whether the <strong>offspring are fertile</strong>, and whether the interbreeding happens in <strong>natural conditions</strong> or only in captivity.</div>

<h2 data-stmt="${R}.s4">5. Examining the definition critically when you are given data <span class="badge teal">required</span></h2>
<p>Exam questions give you information about a particular case and ask you to evaluate whether the organisms are one species, or why the definition is hard to apply. Work through the evidence:</p>
<ol><li><strong>Can the test be done at all?</strong> Sexual reproduction? Organisms alive today? Do the populations ever meet?</li>
<li><strong>Do they interbreed?</strong> In the wild or only in captivity? Is mating physically possible? Does behaviour (song, courtship, timing) keep them apart?</li>
<li><strong>Are the offspring fertile?</strong> All of them, some of them, only one sex? Do hybrids appear in the second generation (the offspring of hybrids)?</li>
<li><strong>What other evidence would help?</strong> Similarity of features, of DNA base sequences (3.1.10.2, 3.1.11.1) or of proteins; whether the populations live and breed separately in nature.</li>
<li><strong>Conclude with care.</strong> State what the data support, and say what is still uncertain. "The data suggest…" is usually better than "This proves…".</li></ol>
<div class="figure"><div class="fig-view">${A.flow()}</div></div>
<div class="notice"><strong>Worked example</strong> ${ILL} Two populations of a moth live in the same valley. One flies at dusk, the other after midnight. In cages, 40 crosses between them gave 1210 caterpillars, of which 1104 became adults; when these hybrids were crossed with each other, no eggs hatched.<br>
<em>Evaluation.</em> The populations can interbreed in captivity and the first-generation hybrids survive well, but the hybrids appear to be infertile (no eggs from hybrid × hybrid crosses hatched), so by the definition they are <strong>separate species</strong>. In the wild, flying at different times (behaviour) would also reduce mating. Limitations: these are caged moths, and only hybrid × hybrid crosses were tested; crossing hybrids back to either parent population, and comparing DNA, would strengthen the conclusion.</div>

<h2 data-stmt="${R}.s4">6. Beyond the specification: other ways to define a species <span class="badge amber">extension</span></h2>
<ul><li>Because the interbreeding test is so often impossible, biologists in practice also recognise species by their <strong>structure</strong> (how museum collections and fossils are classified) and increasingly by <strong>DNA</strong>: groups whose DNA shows they have been evolving separately. These other species concepts are not part of 9610.</li>
<li><strong>Ring species.</strong> Textbooks have described chains of populations in which each population breeds with its neighbour while the two ends of the chain, where they meet, do not interbreed. The classic example given is the herring gull and the lesser black-backed gull around the North Pole. <strong>Treat that example with caution:</strong> a DNA study published in 2004 found that the gull populations do not form a simple ring of interbreeding neighbours (see Biology in Focus). Ring species show how gradual the boundary between species can be, but well-supported cases are rarer and more complicated than the textbook story.</li></ul>`,
    diagrams: [
      { id: "d-3.1.10.1-1", title: "Horse × donkey: why mules are sterile", status: "reviewed", caption: "Horse (2n = 64) and donkey (2n = 62) gametes fuse to give a mule or hinny with 2n = 63. Horse and donkey chromosomes cannot all form homologous pairs in the hybrid's meiosis, so the hybrids are almost always sterile and horses and donkeys are separate species. Schematic: only four chromosomes are drawn.", alt: "Boxes for horse (Equus caballus, 2n = 64) and donkey (Equus asinus, 2n = 62); arrows labelled meiosis to gametes with n = 32 and n = 31; arrows labelled fertilisation to a box for the mule or hinny with 2n = 63. Below, four pairs of bars represent pairing in the hybrid's meiosis, the last bar without a partner, and a box explaining that the hybrid is almost always sterile, so the parents are separate species. A footnote gives mule = male donkey × female horse and hinny = male horse × female donkey.", svg: A.muleCross() },
      { id: "d-3.1.10.1-2", title: "Applying the biological species definition", status: "reviewed", caption: "Questions to ask about any example. The first three decide whether the test can be carried out at all; the last two decide the answer. Even when every answer is yes, fertile hybrids between recognised species mean judgement is still needed.", alt: "A vertical flow chart. Do the organisms reproduce sexually? No: the definition cannot be applied. Are they alive today? No: it cannot be tested. Do they ever have the chance to meet and mate? No: it cannot be tested in nature. Do they interbreed in natural conditions? No: usually separate species, with the caution that dogs of very different sizes are still one species. Are the offspring fertile? No: separate species, as with mules. If yes to all: one species by the definition, but some recognised species produce fertile hybrids and some hybrids are only partly fertile.", svg: A.flow() },
      { id: "d-3.1.10.1-3", title: "Writing a binomial", status: "reviewed", caption: "Genus name with a capital letter, species name in lower case; italics in print, each word underlined when handwritten; the genus may be shortened to its initial after first use.", alt: "The name Panthera leo in large italics, with Panthera labelled genus name, capital first letter, and leo labelled species name, all lower case. Below: in print, italics; handwritten, each word underlined; after first use P. leo; same genus, Panthera tigris for the tiger; never panthera Leo, Panthera Leo or leo on its own.", svg: A.binomial() },
    ],
    interactives: [
      { kind: "spcConcept", title: "Species Concept Challenge", note: "Nine cases, most of them real. For each, read the data and decide whether the biological species definition can be applied reliably, and what it shows. Every answer is marked and recorded; the feedback names the limitation involved.",
        items: challenge },
    ],
    focus: [
      { slot: "mule-hybrid", title: "Mules and hinnies", ref: R, explanation: () => `<p>People have bred mules for thousands of years because they combine the size and strength of the horse with the stamina and sure-footedness of the donkey. A <strong>mule</strong> is the offspring of a male donkey and a female horse; a <strong>hinny</strong> is the offspring of a male horse and a female donkey. Both have 63 chromosomes: 32 from the horse and 31 from the donkey. Because horse and donkey chromosomes differ in number and structure, many cannot pair in meiosis, and mules and hinnies are almost always sterile. Only a little over 60 cases of a female mule producing a foal have been documented since 1527. Every new mule therefore has to be bred from a horse and a donkey, and the two parent species remain separate. Mules show why the species definition asks for <strong>fertile</strong> offspring, not simply for offspring.</p>`,
        question: { id: `q-${R}-fx1`, ref: R, stmts: S(2, 4), type: "short", marks: 3, stem: "A farmer says: 'Horses and donkeys must be the same species, because they can breed together to produce mules.' Use the definition of a species and the information about mules to explain why the farmer is wrong. (3 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "A species is the largest group capable of interbreeding and producing fertile offspring", marks: 1 }, { point: "Mules (and hinnies) are (almost always) sterile / infertile", marks: 1 }, { point: "Because horse and donkey chromosomes (63, different number and structure) cannot all pair in meiosis, so gametes do not form; so horses and donkeys are separate species", marks: 1 }], model: "A species is the largest group of organisms that can interbreed and produce fertile offspring. Horses and donkeys can interbreed, but their offspring, mules, are almost always sterile: a mule has 63 chromosomes, from parents with different numbers and structures of chromosome, so the chromosomes cannot all form homologous pairs in meiosis and gametes do not form. Because the offspring are not fertile, horses and donkeys are separate species.", commonErrors: "Stating that horses and donkeys cannot interbreed (they can); omitting the word fertile." } },
      { slot: "ring-species-gulls", title: "The gull 'ring' that turned out not to be one", ref: R, explanation: () => `<p>The herring gull and the lesser black-backed gull both breed in north-west Europe and rarely interbreed there, so they are treated as separate species. For decades textbooks described them as the two ends of a <strong>ring species</strong>: a chain of gull populations encircling the North Pole, each supposedly interbreeding with its neighbour, so that genes could in principle flow all the way round from one species to the other. In 2004, Dorit Liebers, Peter de Knijff and Andreas Helbig compared mitochondrial DNA from gulls right round the Arctic. They found that the populations do <strong>not</strong> form a simple ring of interbreeding neighbours: the group's history involves populations that spread out from several separate areas and later met, in places hybridising. The gulls remain a striking example of how blurred species boundaries can be, but not of a ring species. The lesson for this topic is that a claim about interbreeding is only as good as the evidence for it, and DNA data can overturn a long-accepted story.</p>`,
        question: { id: `q-${R}-fx2`, ref: R, stmts: S(4, 2), type: "short", marks: 3, stem: "Herring gulls and lesser black-backed gulls breed in the same areas of Europe but rarely interbreed. Some gull populations elsewhere in the Arctic are intermediate in appearance and hybridise with their neighbours. Explain why it is difficult to decide, using the species definition, how many gull species there are. (3 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "Where herring and lesser black-backed gulls meet they rarely interbreed, which suggests separate species", marks: 1 }, { point: "But hybridisation between neighbouring populations means genes may pass between groups / some hybrids may be fertile, so there is no clear boundary", marks: 1 }, { point: "Populations far apart never meet, so whether they could interbreed and produce fertile offspring cannot be tested directly (DNA or other evidence is needed)", marks: 1 }], model: "Where the two gulls breed together they rarely interbreed, which suggests they are separate species. However, other populations hybridise with their neighbours, so genes may pass from group to group and there is no sharp line at which one species ends. Many of the populations live far apart and never meet, so whether they could interbreed and produce fertile offspring cannot be tested directly; other evidence, such as comparisons of DNA, is needed, and it has shown that the populations' history is more complicated than a simple chain.", commonErrors: "Saying that gulls that look different must be different species; ignoring the fertility of hybrids." } },
    ],
    examFocus: `<ul><li>Learn the definition exactly: the <strong>largest group</strong> of organisms <strong>capable of interbreeding</strong> and producing <strong>fertile offspring</strong>. "Can breed together" without "fertile offspring" loses the mark.</li><li>Binomials: <em>Genus species</em>; capital for the genus only; italics in print, underlined by hand; abbreviate the genus only after first use. Practise writing them by hand with each word underlined.</li><li>When given data, check: can the test be done (sexual reproduction, living, ever meet)? do they interbreed in the wild? are the offspring fertile (all, some, one sex)? Then conclude cautiously and say what further evidence (for example DNA comparison) would help.</li><li>Name the limitation precisely: asexual reproduction, extinct or fossil organisms, geographical separation, physical incompatibility, behavioural barriers, fertile hybrids between recognised species, partial fertility, captivity not reflecting the wild.</li><li>Do not say organisms are one species because they look alike, or separate species because they look different: appearance is not the definition.</li><li>Mule sterility: link to meiosis: chromosomes cannot pair as homologous pairs, so gametes do not form.</li></ul>`,
    synoptic: [
      { ref: "3.1.10.2", text: "Species are grouped into genera, families and higher taxa in a hierarchy." },
      { ref: "3.1.9.1", text: "Meiosis needs homologous chromosomes to pair; this is why mules are sterile." },
      { ref: "3.1.11.1", text: "Comparing DNA base sequences helps decide whether populations belong to one species." },
      { ref: "3.1.11.2", text: "Species richness counts species, so how species are defined affects measures of biodiversity." },
      { ref: "3.3.8.2", text: "New species arise when separated populations can no longer interbreed to produce fertile offspring." },
    ],
    quickCheck: [
      { id: `q-${R}-qc1`, ref: R, stmts: S(1), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "What is the basic unit of biological classification?", options: ["The species", "The genus", "The kingdom", "The population"], answer: 0, explanation: "Species are the basic unit; larger taxa are built from species." },
      { id: `q-${R}-qc2`, ref: R, stmts: S(2), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Which is the specification's definition of a species?", options: ["Organisms that look alike", "The largest group of organisms capable of interbreeding and producing fertile offspring", "Organisms that live in the same habitat", "All organisms in the same genus"], answer: 1, explanation: "Interbreeding and fertile offspring, for the largest such group." },
      { id: `q-${R}-qc3`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Which form of the grey wolf's binomial uses capital and lower-case letters correctly?", options: ["Canis Lupus", "canis lupus", "Canis lupus", "Lupus canis"], answer: 2, explanation: "Genus first with a capital letter; species name in lower case (and in print, both words in italics)." },
      { id: `q-${R}-qc4`, ref: R, stmts: S(4, 2), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Why can the species definition not be applied to a bacterium?", options: ["Bacteria are too small to see", "Bacteria reproduce asexually, so interbreeding cannot be tested", "Bacteria have no DNA", "Bacteria are all one species"], answer: 1, explanation: "Asexual reproduction: no interbreeding to test." },
      { id: `q-${R}-qc5`, ref: R, stmts: S(4), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Lions and tigers produce hybrids in zoos; male hybrids are sterile and some females are fertile. This illustrates which difficulty with the species definition?", options: ["Asexual reproduction", "Partial fertility of hybrids, and interbreeding only in captivity", "Extinct organisms", "Physical incompatibility"], answer: 1, explanation: "Fertility is not all or nothing, and captivity may not reflect nature." },
    ],
    flashcards: [
      { id: `f-${R}-1`, front: "What is the basic unit of biological classification?", back: "The species. Larger groups (genus, family and so on) are built from species." },
      { id: `f-${R}-2`, front: "Define a species (specification wording).", back: "The largest group of organisms capable of interbreeding and producing fertile offspring." },
      { id: `f-${R}-3`, front: "Why does the definition say 'fertile' offspring?", back: "Some different species can interbreed but their offspring are sterile (horse × donkey gives a mule), so interbreeding alone does not show two organisms are one species." },
      { id: `f-${R}-4`, front: "What is a binomial?", back: "The two-part scientific name of a species: genus name then species name, e.g. Homo sapiens." },
      { id: `f-${R}-5`, front: "Rules for writing a binomial.", back: "Genus first with a capital letter; species name in lower case; italics in print, each word underlined when handwritten; genus may be abbreviated (P. leo) after first use." },
      { id: `f-${R}-6`, front: "Why are binomials used rather than common names?", back: "They are universal: the same name in every country and language. Common names vary and can refer to different species (British robin v American robin)." },
      { id: `f-${R}-7`, front: "Panthera leo and Panthera tigris: what does the shared first word tell you?", back: "Both species are in the same genus, Panthera, so they are closely related." },
      { id: `f-${R}-8`, front: "Mule and hinny: parents and chromosome numbers.", back: "Mule: male donkey × female horse; hinny: male horse × female donkey. Horse 2n = 64, donkey 2n = 62, hybrid 2n = 63." },
      { id: `f-${R}-9`, front: "Why are mules almost always sterile?", back: "Horse and donkey chromosomes differ in number and structure, so they cannot all pair as homologous chromosomes in meiosis; viable gametes rarely form." },
      { id: `f-${R}-10`, front: "Why can the species definition not be applied to organisms that reproduce asexually?", back: "They reproduce without mating, so there is no interbreeding to test (bacteria, all-female whiptail lizards, many dandelions)." },
      { id: `f-${R}-11`, front: "Why is the definition hard to apply to fossils?", back: "Extinct organisms cannot be bred; species have to be judged from structure (and rarely ancient DNA)." },
      { id: `f-${R}-12`, front: "How does geographical separation cause a problem?", back: "Populations that never meet cannot be observed interbreeding; matings in captivity may not show what would happen in the wild." },
      { id: `f-${R}-13`, front: "Give an example of physical incompatibility within a species.", back: "Chihuahua and Great Dane cannot mate naturally, but both are domestic dogs: genes flow between them through dogs of intermediate size." },
      { id: `f-${R}-14`, front: "How can behaviour stop interbreeding? Give an example.", back: "Different songs, displays or breeding times. The chiffchaff and willow warbler look almost identical but sing different songs and rarely hybridise." },
      { id: `f-${R}-15`, front: "Give an example of fertile hybrids between recognised species.", back: "Polar bear × brown bear (wild hybrids, some of which have bred); grey wolf × coyote; many plants." },
      { id: `f-${R}-16`, front: "Why is appearance a poor guide to species?", back: "Look-alikes can be separate species, and members of one species can look very different (males and females, larvae and adults, breeds of dog)." },
      { id: `f-${R}-17`, front: "Ring species: what should you remember? (extension)", back: "The herring gull example in many textbooks was contradicted by a 2004 DNA study; ring species are rarer and more complicated than the textbook story. Not required by 9610." },
    ],
    questions: [
      // ---- MCQ ----
      { id: `q-${R}-m1`, ref: R, stmts: S(1), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Which statement explains why the species is regarded as the basic unit of classification?", options: ["It is the largest group in the hierarchy", "It is the smallest of the main groups, and the higher groups are made up of species", "It always contains only one population", "It is defined only by appearance"], answer: 1, explanation: "Every organism is placed in a species first, and genera, families and higher taxa are groupings of species." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m2`, ref: R, stmts: S(2), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Two plants are placed in the same species. Which observation would best support this?", options: ["They have flowers of the same colour", "They grow in the same field", "They can cross to produce seeds that grow into fertile plants", "They are pollinated by the same insect"], answer: 2, explanation: "Interbreeding to give fertile offspring is the test in the definition." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m3`, ref: R, stmts: S(2, 4), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Horses and donkeys are separate species because:", options: ["they cannot mate", "they look different", "their offspring are almost always sterile", "they live in different countries"], answer: 2, explanation: "They can interbreed, but the offspring are not fertile." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m4`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "A student handwrites the binomial of the tiger in an exam. Which is correct?", options: ["Panthera tigris, with each word underlined separately", "PANTHERA TIGRIS, in capitals", "Panthera Tigris, with neither word underlined", "tigris Panthera, with only the first word underlined"], answer: 0, explanation: "Genus first with a capital; species name in lower case; in handwriting each word is underlined (italics in print)." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m5`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "In the binomial <em>Apis mellifera</em> (the western honey bee), <em>Apis</em> is the name of the:", options: ["species", "family", "genus", "order"], answer: 2, explanation: "The first word of a binomial is the genus." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m6`, ref: R, stmts: S(3, 1), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Using only their binomials, which pair is most closely related?", options: ["Felis catus (domestic cat) and Felis silvestris (wildcat)", "Felis catus and Panthera leo (lion)", "Panthera leo and Canis lupus (grey wolf)", "Canis lupus and Felis silvestris"], answer: 0, explanation: "Only the first pair share a genus, Felis." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m7`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Why do scientists use binomials rather than common names?", options: ["Binomials are shorter", "A binomial is the same in every country and language and refers to only one species", "Common names are always wrong", "Binomials describe the colour of the organism"], answer: 1, explanation: "Binomials are universal and unambiguous." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m8`, ref: R, stmts: S(4), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "A horse has 2n = 64 and a donkey 2n = 62. How many chromosomes are in the body cells of a mule?", options: ["31", "62", "63", "126"], answer: 2, explanation: "32 (horse gamete) + 31 (donkey gamete) = 63." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m9`, ref: R, stmts: S(4, 2), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Which explains why mules are almost always sterile?", options: ["Mules have no reproductive organs", "Horse and donkey chromosomes cannot all form homologous pairs in meiosis, so viable gametes rarely form", "Mules have too many chromosomes to fit in a nucleus", "Mitosis cannot occur in mule cells"], answer: 1, explanation: "Pairing of homologous chromosomes in meiosis fails." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m10`, ref: R, stmts: S(4), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "To which organism is the species definition most difficult to apply?", options: ["A cat", "A bacterium that reproduces by binary fission", "A sunflower that is pollinated by bees", "A frog"], answer: 1, explanation: "Asexual reproduction: no interbreeding." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m11`, ref: R, stmts: S(4), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Chihuahuas and Great Danes cannot mate naturally, but they are the same species. This shows that:", options: ["the definition must be applied to the whole interbreeding group, because physical incompatibility can prevent mating within a species", "dogs reproduce asexually", "they produce sterile offspring", "dogs are classified by appearance alone"], answer: 0, explanation: "Genes flow between them through dogs of intermediate size." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m12`, ref: R, stmts: S(4, 2), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Wild hybrids of polar bears and brown bears have themselves produced cubs. What does this show?", options: ["Polar bears and brown bears must be one species", "Some recognised species can produce fertile hybrids, so the definition does not always give a clear answer", "Bears reproduce asexually", "The hybrids are sterile"], answer: 1, explanation: "Fertile hybrids between recognised species are a limitation of the definition." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m13`, ref: R, stmts: S(4), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Two fossil forms of an extinct snail differ slightly in shell shape. Why is it difficult to decide whether they were separate species?", options: ["Shells do not fossilise", "Whether they could interbreed and produce fertile offspring cannot be tested", "Snails are always one species", "Fossils contain too much DNA"], answer: 1, explanation: "Extinct organisms cannot be bred; structure must be used instead." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m14`, ref: R, stmts: S(4), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Two populations of a lizard live on different islands. In captivity they produce fertile offspring. What is the main limitation of using this result to classify them?", options: ["Lizards cannot be bred in captivity", "They never meet in the wild, and animals in captivity may mate with partners they would not choose in nature", "Fertile offspring show they are different species", "Lizards reproduce only asexually"], answer: 1, explanation: "Geographical separation means the test cannot be done in natural conditions." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m15`, ref: R, stmts: S(4), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Two kinds of cricket look identical, live in the same fields and produce fertile hybrids in the laboratory, but in the field females move only towards the call of their own kind and hybrids are almost never found. The best conclusion is:", options: ["they are one species because they produce fertile hybrids", "they behave as separate species in nature: behaviour (the call) prevents interbreeding, although the laboratory result shows the boundary is not absolute", "they reproduce asexually", "they must be separate species because they look different"], answer: 1, explanation: "Behavioural barriers can keep species apart in nature; laboratory crosses may not reflect natural behaviour." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m16`, ref: R, stmts: S(1, 3), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Which statement is correct?", options: ["Every species belongs to a genus, and its binomial begins with that genus name", "Two species can have identical binomials if they live on different continents", "A genus is smaller than a species", "The species name is written with a capital letter"], answer: 0, explanation: "Each binomial is unique and begins with the genus name." },
      // ---- gap ----
      { id: `q-${R}-g1`, ref: R, stmts: S(2), type: "gap", marks: 3, stem: "Complete the definition.", text: "A species is the {{1}} group of organisms capable of {{2}} and producing {{3}} offspring.", answers: { 1: ["largest"], 2: ["interbreeding", "interbreed", "breeding together"], 3: ["fertile"] }, explanation: "Largest; interbreeding; fertile." },
      { id: `q-${R}-g2`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "gap", marks: 3, stem: "The house sparrow is <em>Passer domesticus</em>.", text: "Passer is the name of the {{1}}. domesticus is the {{2}} name. After the full name has been used once, it may be written as {{3}} domesticus.", answers: { 1: ["genus"], 2: ["species", "specific"], 3: ["P.", "P"] }, explanation: "Genus; species (the specific epithet); P. domesticus." },
      // ---- matching ----
      { id: `q-${R}-mt1`, ref: R, stmts: S(4), type: "match", marks: 3, stem: "Match each example to the difficulty it shows in applying the species definition.", pairs: [{ left: "An all-female lizard whose eggs develop without fertilisation", right: "Asexual reproduction" }, { left: "Two forms of fossil trilobite", right: "Extinct organisms cannot be bred" }, { left: "A Chihuahua and a Great Dane", right: "Physical incompatibility within a species" }, { left: "Frog populations on islands 300 km apart", right: "Geographical separation" }, { left: "Two look-alike warblers with different songs", right: "Behaviour prevents mating" }, { left: "Polar bear × brown bear hybrids that breed", right: "Fertile hybrids between recognised species" }] },
      // ---- short answer and data ----
      { id: `q-${R}-s1`, ref: R, stmts: S(1, 2), type: "short", marks: 2, stem: "Explain why the species, rather than the genus or the family, is described as the basic unit of classification. (2 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "Species is the smallest / lowest of the main groups (taxa) / every organism is placed in a species first", marks: 1 }, { point: "Higher taxa (genus, family and above) are made up of / built from species; accept instead: a species can be defined by interbreeding to produce fertile offspring, whereas the limits of higher taxa are decided by biologists", marks: 1 }], model: "The species is the smallest of the main groups, and every organism is first placed in a species. The higher groups are then built up from species: a genus is a group of similar species, a family a group of similar genera, and so on. (Many textbooks add that a species can be defined by interbreeding to produce fertile offspring, whereas the limits of higher groups are decided by biologists; this is also creditworthy but not required.)", commonErrors: "Saying the species is the largest group." },
      { id: `q-${R}-s2`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "short", marks: 2, stem: "A student wrote in an essay: 'The lion, panthera Leo, is closely related to the tiger, Tigris.' Identify two errors in how the names are written and give the correct forms. (2 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "Genus needs a capital and species name lower case: Panthera leo (italic or underlined)", marks: 1 }, { point: "The species name alone is not a binomial; the tiger is Panthera tigris (genus required, lower-case species name)", marks: 1 }], model: "The lion's name should be Panthera leo, with a capital P for the genus and a lower-case l for the species name (in italics, or underlined when handwritten). 'Tigris' on its own is not a name: the tiger's binomial is Panthera tigris, genus first, species name in lower case.", commonErrors: "Correcting only capitalisation and not the missing genus." },
      { id: `q-${R}-s3`, ref: R, stmts: S(4, 2), type: "short", marks: 3, stem: `Data ${ILL}. A zoo kept records of hybrids between lions and tigers.\n<table class="tbl"><thead><tr><th>Hybrids</th><th>Number studied</th><th>Number that produced offspring</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Male</td><td>14</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>Female</td><td>11</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table>Evaluate whether these data support the view that lions and tigers are separate species. (3 marks)`, scheme: [{ point: "Lions and tigers can interbreed (hybrids exist) and some female hybrids are fertile, which on its own would suggest one species", marks: 1 }, { point: "No male hybrids were fertile and most females were not, so hybrid fertility is low / partial, supporting separate species", marks: 1 }, { point: "Limitation: hybrids are from captivity, which may not reflect the wild, where lions and tigers do not interbreed; small sample size", marks: 1 }], model: "Lions and tigers can interbreed, and three of the eleven female hybrids produced offspring, so some hybrids are fertile, which on its own might suggest a single species. However, none of the 14 males and only about a quarter of the females were fertile, so hybrid fertility is very low and only partial, which supports treating them as separate species. The data come from a zoo: in the wild lions and tigers do not interbreed, and the samples are small, so the conclusion is supported but not certain.", commonErrors: "Concluding one species because some hybrids are fertile, without considering the males or captivity." },
      { id: `q-${R}-s4`, ref: R, stmts: S(4), type: "short", marks: 2, stem: "Explain why the species definition cannot be used to classify (a) bdelloid rotifers, tiny freshwater animals in which no males have ever been found, and (b) dinosaurs. (2 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "(a) They reproduce asexually / without males, so there is no interbreeding to test", marks: 1 }, { point: "(b) They are extinct (known from fossils), so interbreeding and fertility of offspring cannot be tested", marks: 1 }], model: "(a) Bdelloid rotifers reproduce only asexually, so they never interbreed and the test in the definition cannot be carried out. (b) Dinosaurs are extinct and known only from fossils, so whether they could interbreed and produce fertile offspring can never be observed.", commonErrors: "Saying rotifers are too small to classify." },
      { id: `q-${R}-s5`, ref: R, stmts: S(4, 2), type: "short", marks: 4, stem: `Data ${ILL}. Two populations of a songbird, E and F, breed in neighbouring forests. Scientists played recorded songs to territorial males and recorded whether they responded.\n<table class="tbl"><thead><tr><th>Males tested</th><th>Responded to song of own population / %</th><th>Responded to song of other population / %</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Population E (n = 40)</td><td>93</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>Population F (n = 36)</td><td>89</td><td>11</td></tr></tbody></table>In an aviary, birds from E and F paired and raised fertile young. Evaluate the claim that E and F are separate species. (4 marks)`, scheme: [{ point: "Males respond mainly to their own population's song (about 90 % v about 10 %)", marks: 1 }, { point: "So in the wild song / behaviour probably reduces mating between the populations, supporting separate species", marks: 1 }, { point: "But in the aviary they interbred and produced fertile young, which by the definition suggests one species / the barrier is not absolute", marks: 1 }, { point: "Limitation: aviary may not reflect the wild; response of males is not the same as female mate choice / need field data on hybrids or DNA comparison", marks: 1 }], model: "Most males of each population responded to their own population's song (93 % and 89 %) but few responded to the other's (8 % and 11 %), so song is likely to act as a behavioural barrier that reduces interbreeding in the wild, supporting the claim. However, in the aviary the two populations paired and produced fertile young, so they are capable of interbreeding and producing fertile offspring, which by the definition suggests one species. The aviary may not reflect natural conditions, and male responses do not show directly which partners females choose, so field data on how often hybrids occur, or a comparison of their DNA, are needed before deciding.", commonErrors: "Ignoring the aviary result, or treating it as decisive." },
      { id: `q-${R}-d1`, ref: R, stmts: S(4, 2), type: "short", marks: 3, stem: "Data. Neanderthals became extinct about 40 000 years ago. Analysis of DNA from Neanderthal bones shows that the genomes of present-day people with ancestry outside Africa contain about 2 % of DNA inherited from Neanderthals. (a) What does this suggest about Neanderthals and modern humans? (2 marks) (b) Suggest why biologists disagree about whether Neanderthals were a separate species. (1 mark)", scheme: [{ point: "(a) Neanderthals and modern humans interbred", marks: 1 }, { point: "(a) And at least some offspring were fertile (their DNA has been passed down to people living today)", marks: 1 }, { point: "(b) They are extinct, so interbreeding cannot be tested directly / the evidence is indirect; hybrids being fertile suggests one species, but large differences in structure (skeleton) suggest separate species", marks: 1 }], model: "(a) Neanderthal DNA could only reach present-day people if Neanderthals and modern humans interbred and at least some of the hybrid offspring were fertile, passing the DNA on through later generations. (b) Because Neanderthals are extinct, the species definition cannot be applied directly; the fertile hybrids suggest one species, but Neanderthals differed considerably in skeleton, so biologists weigh the evidence differently.", commonErrors: "Saying modern humans evolved from Neanderthals. Confusing the share of present-day genomes inherited from Neanderthals (about 2 %) with how similar Neanderthal and modern human DNA is overall (about 99.7 % identical)." },
      // ---- extended ----
      { id: `q-${R}-e1`, ref: R, stmts: S(1, 2, 4), type: "extended", marks: 6, stem: "Extended response. Species are the basic unit of classification and are often defined as the largest group of organisms capable of interbreeding and producing fertile offspring. Evaluate this definition, using named examples. (6 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "Strength: ties species to a natural process (interbreeding / shared gene pool); works for many sexually reproducing organisms", marks: 1 }, { point: "Fertile offspring requirement explained, e.g. horse × donkey gives sterile mules, so separate species", marks: 1 }, { point: "Cannot be applied to asexual organisms (bacteria, all-female lizards) or to extinct / fossil organisms", marks: 1 }, { point: "Cannot be tested for geographically separated populations; captivity results may mislead", marks: 1 }, { point: "Physical incompatibility (Chihuahua / Great Dane) or behaviour (song, timing) can prevent mating, so mating ability is not straightforward to judge", marks: 1 }, { point: "Fertile or partly fertile hybrids between recognised species (polar × brown bear; ligers) blur boundaries; conclusion: useful but other evidence (structure, DNA) needed", marks: 1 }], levels: "Level 3 (5–6): balanced evaluation with strengths and at least three distinct limitations, each with an appropriate named example, and a justified conclusion. Level 2 (3–4): definition explained with two or three limitations and some examples. Level 1 (1–2): definition stated with one limitation or examples without explanation.", model: "The definition has the great strength of linking a species to something that happens in nature: members of a species can interbreed, so they share a pool of genes, and the definition works well for most sexually reproducing animals and plants. Its requirement for fertile offspring is important: horses and donkeys interbreed, but mules are almost always sterile because horse and donkey chromosomes cannot pair in meiosis, so the two are correctly treated as separate species. However, the definition often cannot be applied. Organisms that reproduce only asexually, such as bacteria or the all-female New Mexico whiptail lizard, reproduce without mating, and extinct organisms known from fossils cannot be tested. Populations that are geographically separated never have the chance to interbreed, and matings in captivity may not show what would happen in the wild. Even when organisms meet, mating may be prevented within one species by physical incompatibility, as between a Chihuahua and a Great Dane, while look-alike birds such as the chiffchaff and willow warbler are kept apart by their songs. Finally, some recognised species produce fertile hybrids, such as polar bears and brown bears, or partly fertile ones, such as lion × tiger hybrids, so the boundary is not always sharp. The definition is therefore a useful principle, but in practice biologists must also use evidence from structure and from DNA.", commonErrors: "Listing limitations without examples; stating the definition without 'fertile'; concluding the definition is useless." },
    ],
  };
})();
